course: medicine
lesson: 63
title: Famous athletes and what being a professional takes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Being a pro is mostly the boring parts done for years: practice, sleep, planned food, rest days, physio after injuries, and a plan for after a career that usually ends in the 30s.
explain: Tyler, 9, in Manchester: «Got scouted, bro. Academy at 10, City at 18, no cap» (no lie); his coach smiles, «Enjoy it, lad, just keep doing your homework an' all» (as well), because the figure often quoted is that under 1 in 100 academy boys end up as professionals.
explain: Stars had setbacks too: as a child Lionel Messi was diagnosed with a growth hormone problem, joined Barcelona at 13 and was helped with his treatment, so being small at 10 doesn't decide your future.
explain: Pain is a message, not a badge: sore knees or heels in a growth spurt need rest and a check, a torn knee ligament means surgery and months out, and when Simone Biles withdrew from events in Tokyo to protect herself, many people called it brave.
fact: Only a tiny share of boys who join a football academy young become professionals; the figure often quoted is under 1 in 100.
fact: The «10,000 hours» idea was made popular by a book; practice matters a lot, but there is no magic number, and coaching, rest and enjoyment matter too.
fact: In children who do lots of sport, growing bones and tendons can get sore, often at the knee or heel during growth spurts, and pushing through can make it worse; tired muscles after training are normal, but sharp or lasting pain needs rest and a check.
fact: A torn ACL, a ligament inside the knee, often needs surgery and around 9 months of rehab before a player is back.
fact: Most professional footballers retire in their 30s, and many outside the top leagues earn ordinary wages and need a second career.
fact: As a child, Lionel Messi was diagnosed with a growth hormone problem; he joined Barcelona's academy at 13, and the club helped with his treatment.
fact: Serena Williams won 23 Grand Slam singles titles after years of training from a young age.
fact: Muscles repair and get stronger during rest and sleep, so professional athletes plan their sleep like their training.
fact: Tour de France riders race 21 stages with 2 rest days and eat large, carefully planned amounts, with food and drink on the bike.
fact: At the Tokyo Olympics, Simone Biles withdrew from events, saying she had to look after her mental health and had «the twisties», losing track of where she was in the air; at Paris 2024 she won 3 golds and a silver.
fact: Children born early in the school year can be nearly a year older than classmates, so they are often bigger and get picked more; many younger ones catch up later.
fact: «Minted» is British slang for very rich, «ken» is Scots for you know, and «eejit» is Irish for idiot.
exercise: Tyler: «Academy at 10, City at 18, no cap». What's the often-quoted chance? | Under 1 in 100 make it | About 9 in 10 make it | Everyone in an academy makes it | About half make it
exercise: If about 1 in 100 make it, how many pros from 200 academy kids? | About 2 | About 20 | About 100 | About 200
exercise: Isla: «10,000 hours and I'll be a genius, it's science, ken». What's true? | It's a rough idea, not a rule | It's a proven law for everyone | 100 hours is enough | Hours don't matter at all
exercise: Ben limps: «Knees are killin' me, no pain no gain, innit?» What should he do? | Tell his coach and a parent, and rest | Train harder through the pain to toughen up | Hide it from everyone until the season ends | Keep running on it until the knee goes numb
exercise: Why do sporty kids' knees or heels get sore in growth spurts? | Growing bones and tendons are under strain | Their bones stop growing | Their boots are too new | They eat too much fruit
exercise: Chloe: «It's just a knock». Her striker tore a knee ligament. How long out, often? | About 9 months | About 9 days | About 9 weeks | Just one match
exercise: Arjun: «Footballers are minted for life, so why GCSEs?» (the big exams at 16) What's true? | Careers are short and most earn far less | All pros are rich forever | Footballers play till 70 | School doesn't matter for anyone who's good at sport
exercise: When do most pro footballers retire? | In their 30s | In their 50s | In their 20s at the latest | They never retire
exercise: Lucía feels too short. What does Messi's story show? | Being small at 10 doesn't decide your future | Only tall players can succeed | Short players never get scouted | Height is all that matters
exercise: How old was Messi when he joined Barcelona's academy? | 13 | 5 | 18 | 21
exercise: Maya: «Serena was just born good, y'all». What built her 23 titles? | Years of training on top of talent | Talent alone, with hardly any practice | Luck in every final | A special racket
exercise: Connor: «Pros don't sleep, they just train, so they do». What's true? | Pros plan sleep like training | Pros sleep 3 hours a night and train the rest | Sleep makes muscles weaker, so pros skip it | Only lazy pros sleep more than a few hours
exercise: Sipho: «They're riding all day, they must eat whatever, hey?» What do Tour riders do? | Eat lots of carefully planned food | Eat only sweets and gels while on the bike | Eat nothing at all during the long stages | Eat whatever junk they like, they burn it off
exercise: Grace: «Why'd Simone Biles pull out in Tokyo? So weak». What did Biles say? | She had to look after her mental health | She was bored of gymnastics | She wanted a holiday | She'd already won everything and wanted to rest
exercise: What did Biles win at Paris 2024? | 3 golds and a silver | Nothing at all, she'd retired | 1 bronze | 10 golds
exercise: Ethan, born in August, wasn't picked. Why are September kids often picked more? | They can be nearly a year older | September is a lucky month for footballers | August kids can't kick as hard, it's science | Coaches just like autumn birthdays better
test: Tyler's coach says «keep doing your homework an' all». Why? | Almost all academy players are let go at some point | Homework makes you a better striker | Academies only take top spellers | Coaches like checking homework
test: Isla does 3 hours a day for a year. About how many hours is that? | About 1,095 | About 365 | About 3,000 | About 10,000
test: Ben's friend: «Tired muscles and sharp pain are the same, just ignore both». Correct it. | Tired is normal, sharp or lasting pain needs rest and a check | Both always mean a broken bone | Both must be ignored | Sharp pain means you're getting stronger
test: Chloe's dad: «That's his knee ligament, love. Long time out». What will the player likely need? | Surgery, physio and patience | Just a cold spray and back Saturday | A new pair of boots | Nothing, pros walk it off
test: What would happen if Arjun dropped school and wasn't picked by 18? | He'd have fewer choices for a career | He'd be picked automatically later | Clubs would pay him anyway because he'd trained hard | Nothing, everyone turns pro
test: Connor's coach says «Ah, you eejit». What does «eejit» mean? | Idiot | Genius | Goalkeeper | Coach
test: Lucía's brother shows a documentary: «He was tiny too, and look». What helped Messi as a child? | Treatment for a growth hormone problem | Sleeping 20 hours a day | Being the tallest boy in his class at school | Never playing until 18
test: Grace's mum: «Not weak, love. Brave». Why might stopping be brave? | Knowing when you're not safe to go on is strength | Stopping always means giving up | Brave people never stop | Biles was told to stop by fans
test: Ethan: «I'm just mid at footy». What's likely true? | Being younger in the year isn't being worse | August kids never catch up, whatever they do in training | Being picked at 10 decides everything that comes later | Only September kids ever go on to become pros
test: A cartoon star eats burgers all day and wins the Tour. What's real? | Riders' food is planned to fuel and recover | Junk food is best for cycling | Riders don't need to eat | Burgers are banned on bikes
test: Maya says Serena was «just born good». Which is a fair reply? | Talent starts you off; practice keeps you going | Practice can never beat talent | Serena never practised | Talent and practice are the same
exam: Identify the proportion of young academy footballers frequently cited as attaining professional status. | Fewer than one in one hundred | Approximately nine in ten | Approximately one half | Every participant without exception
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the notion of ten thousand hours of practice? | It is an approximate idea rather than a fixed rule | It is an established law of nature | It guarantees world-class performance | It applies exclusively to football
exam: It is given that a child experiences persistent pain in the knee during a period of rapid growth. Select the appropriate course of action. | Rest and assessment by an appropriate adult | Intensified training without interruption or complaint | Concealment of the pain from the coach | Continued play until the pain subsides
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which professional athletes regulate their sleep. | Muscles are repaired and strengthened during rest | Sleep diminishes muscular strength | Sleep is prohibited during competition by the governing bodies | Sleep replaces the need for nourishment
